  draw_debug_overlay always drew the green circle when a blob was found. The
  real bug was in the detector's filtering: a real LED highlight on your
  camera only lights up a handful of pixels (~3px radius) with a somewhat
  ragged edge (circularity ~0.54), but the thresholds required radius ≥ 4px
  and circularity ≥ 0.6, so the actual LED was being thrown out every time —
  exactly what you saw as "no circle around the light."

  I verified this against a live frame from your webcam (camera index 5) with
  the LED lit, confirmed the rejection, then:
  - Lowered the minimum blob radius (4→2px) and circularity threshold
    (0.6→0.35) to match what a real small LED highlight actually looks like.
  - Changed the blob-selection logic to prefer the *roundest* candidate rather
    than the *largest*, since point-light sources are compact while
    window/surface glare patches are big but irregular — otherwise the looser
    thresholds would've just caused it to lock onto glare instead.
  - Added a minimum on-screen circle radius (10px) purely for visibility, so a
    3px real detection doesn't draw a circle too tiny to actually see — the
    underlying detected radius used for the aiming math is untouched.

